{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RmrkhyD6hi6hJV8qXrRsBk","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough analysis of the Almanac codebase, I'll now compile my findings into concrete deepening candidates. Let me prepare a comprehensive report:\n\n## ARCHITECTURAL FRICTION SURVEY: Almanac Codebase\n\nI've conducted a detailed end-to-end probe of the major modules and identified **6 concrete deepening candidates** where the seam pattern is shallow, duplication hides, or pure functions fragment real bugs.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Ledger + Rubric Machinery: Shallow Separation (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 71–340, 459–773)\n\n**Problem:** The rubric (lines 71–210) and findings ledger (lines 459–773) are presented as distinct concerns but share deep coupling: the ledger's finding adjudication (lines 902–969) **appends to the rubric** (line 932: `almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion`), and the rubric's acceptance checklist (lines 375–381) **gates the ledger's convergence predicate** (lines 1370–1384). Yet they are implemented as 30+ separate functions across 700 lines in the same file.\n\nThe interface:\n- Rubric: write/approve/acceptance/append-criterion/snapshot/verify (6 public, ~70 lines of actual work)\n- Ledger: path/init/has/append/parse/record/open-blocking/status/set-status/set-status-many (10 public, ~200 lines)  \nPlus their adjudication coupling (lines 917–968: `almanac_harden_ratify` branches on whether the ledger is empty, then appends to both).\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/harden-findings.sh`** module that owns both rubric and ledger as a unit. Rename the pair as **\"finding tracker\"** — the real domain concept they represent together. One module with a clean contract:\n```\nalmanac_harden_tracker_init(root, target)\nalmanac_harden_tracker_rubric_acceptance_met(root, target)\nalmanac_harden_tracker_open_blocking(root, target)\nalmanac_harden_tracker_ratify_and_record(root, target, id, lens, ..., reproduces) → blocks/note/reopened/dropped\nalmanac_harden_tracker_append_criterion(root, target, criterion)\n```\nRemove the dual snapshot/verify guards from harden-core.sh; move them into the tracker's ratify verb.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Rubric + ledger mutations are now co-located, making the append-during-ratify pattern obvious.\n- **Leverage:** Tests of \"when a finding reproduces, the bar grows\" are now trivial (one module call).\n- **Test impact:** The current 17 rubric + 8 ledger functions collapse into ~8 tracker functions with a unified contract; tests shift from testing dual interfaces to testing one coherent state machine.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Dashboard State + Render Split (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 1719–1949)\n\n**Problem:** The dashboard is split into two pure functions:\n- `almanac_harden_dashboard_rows` (lines 1766–1787): pure render — reads stdin, emits printable rows.\n- `almanac_harden_dashboard_worker_rows` (lines 1793–1815): gather worker TSV — reads live run state, feeds the pure render.\n\nYet between them lives **implicit knowledge**: the gathering happens in terms of (id/provider/health) TSV rows that the pure render hardcodes (line 1777: `while IFS=$'\\t' read -r id provider health`). If a caller wants to add a fourth column (e.g., `started_at`) to the dashboard, they must edit both functions in sync — the contract is implicit in the TSV tab count.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/harden-dashboard.sh`** module. Define the TSV schema explicitly (via a field-list function, like `almanac_loop_record_fields` in run.sh):\n```\nalmanac_harden_dashboard_worker_schema() { printf '%s\\n' id provider health; }\n```\nThe gather function yields lines matching that schema; the render iterates the schema when parsing each line. A caller adding a fourth field edits exactly one place.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The schema lives in one place; breaches are immediate.\n- **Leverage:** Future dashboards (converge, ralph) can reuse the TSV composition pattern.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"when a worker is stalled, glyph is *\" are pure table-driven, not shell parsing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Ratification Seam + Demo Reproducer Abstraction (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 787–883)\n\n**Problem:** The ratification logic (lines 902–969: `almanac_harden_ratify`) uses `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` (lines 847–883) as an execution seam. That's good — tests can override the seam. But `demo_reproduces` **does two jobs**: (1) runs the conductor agent (lines 868–876), and (2) parses the verdict from its result (lines 878–881).\n\nA bug hiding here: if the conductor's message is malformed, the parser returns \"not\" (line 829, conservative default). But a stale agent that crashes mid-work returns \"not\" *identically*, making the two failure modes indistinguishable in a test. A test can assert \"verdict is not\" but cannot distinguish \"LLM said not\" from \"runner crashed.\"\n\n**Solution:** Split `demo_reproduces` into two seams:\n```\nalmanac_harden_conductor_verdict(prompt, target) → {reproduces|not}  [the pure parser]\nalmanac_harden_demo_reproduces(demo, target, provider, ...) → 0|1  [the orchestrator]\n```\nThe second calls the conductor via `agent_capture`, then calls the first on the result. Tests override `_verdict` to drive the parser's logic independently of the agent; integration tests override both.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Parsing logic is now in the verdict parser, not buried in the reproducer.\n- **Leverage:** Any loop needing LLM-based yes/no verdicts (e.g., harden's overseer, converge's overseer) can reuse the pure parser.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"a garbled response is conservative\" are now pure string-based, not mocking an agent run. The agent integration is tested separately.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Converge Dirty-Path Tracking: O(N²) Anti-Pattern (converge-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 655–708)\n\n**Problem:** `almanac_converge_commit_agent_paths` (line 674) computes agent-touched paths as `(dirty-after) - (dirty-before)` using `comm -23` on two sorted lists. But dirty-before is captured BEFORE the agent runs (line 755); after the agent, the code recomputes dirty-after (line 678) by re-shelling `git diff` + `git ls-files`.\n\nOn a repo with 10K tracked files, this happens once per round. On a 100-round converge, that's 100× re-listing the entire tree. The seam is:\n```bash\npre_dirty=\"$(almanac_converge_dirty_paths \"$root\")\"   # line 755\n# ... agent runs ...\npost_dirty=\"$(almanac_converge_dirty_paths \"$root\")\"  # line 678\nagent_paths=\"$(comm -23 <(printf '%s\\n' \"$post_dirty\") <(printf '%s\\n' \"$pre_dirty\")\"  # line 681\n```\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/converge-state.sh`** (or fold into converge-core.sh) with a minimal seam:\n```\nalmanac_converge_apply_agent_edits(root, slug, round, pre_dirty_snapshot)  [all-in-one]\n```\nInternally: capture pre-dirty once at round start, compute post-dirty once, diff once, commit once. The round loop (lines 916–981) calls this once per round instead of twice (commit + report).\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The git-state machine lives in one place; rounds that don't commit don't re-list.\n- **Leverage:** Future loops (ralph-in-converge) that also track agent edits reuse this.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"agent touched file X\" mock one seam, not two.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Provider Name Leakage: Adapters vs. Default Selection (lib/agent.sh + lib/providers/claude.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/agent.sh`, `/lib/providers/claude.sh`, `/lib/role.sh`\n\n**Problem:** The provider seam is well-designed: no `if provider == \"claude\"` outside the adapter. But the **default selection** (CONTEXT.md line 23: \"claude → codex\" order) lives in `lib/agent.sh` or `lib/role.sh`, not in a provider itself. If a new provider (e.g., `grok`) should be *first* in the preference order, the fix requires editing the central default-selection code, not adding one file.\n\nCheck:\n```bash\ngrep -n \"default.*provider\\|preference.*order\\|claude.*codex\" /lib/*.sh\n```\nThis seam is currently in `role.sh` or `agent.sh`, not declaratively in the providers themselves.\n\n**Solution:** Add a **priority field** to each provider adapter:\n```bash\nalmanac_provider_<name>_priority() { printf '%s\\n' 100; }  # in each provider\n```\nThen `lib/agent.sh` discovers all providers, sorts by priority, and selects the first available. Adding a `grok` provider with priority 50 automatically makes it first without touching central code.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Provider preference order is now declared in each provider file, not centralized.\n- **Leverage:** A fork or extension that adds a provider never edits core seam logic.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"default provider selection picks claude when available\" become table-driven: load all providers, sort, check first available.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Loop Adapter New-Run Composition: Duplicated Key/Val Parsing (lib/loops/harden.sh, converge.sh, ralph.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/loops/harden.sh` (lines 97–131), `/lib/loops/converge.sh` (lines 167–220), `/lib/loops/ralph.sh` (lines 127–159)\n\n**Problem:** All three loop adapters implement the same pattern:\n```bash\n# Lines 99–105 (harden), 170–180 (converge), 130–141 (ralph):\nfor kv in \"$@\"; do\n  key=\"${kv%%=*}\"; val=\"${kv#*=}\"\n  case \"$key\" in\n    provider) provider=\"$val\" ;;\n    model) model=\"$val\" ;;\n    ...\n  esac\ndone\n```\n\nThis is pure duplication. If a new config field is added to the `new_run_argv` / `new_run_env` contract (e.g., a `timeout` parameter), the fix requires editing three files in lockstep.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/loop-config.sh`** module with a single key/val unpacker:\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_config_parse_kv(kv_pairs_array) → sets ALMANAC_CONFIG_<FIELD> vars\n```\nOr a simpler approach: a `while` loop wrapper that the adapter sources. Ralph uses it:\n```bash\n_almanac_parse_kv provider prd mode iterations rounds ... < <(printf '%s\\n' \"$@\")\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The kv→variable mapping lives in one place.\n- **Leverage:** New loops (if any) reuse the parser; new fields don't require 3-file edits.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"new_run_argv with provider=codex sets CONVERGE_PROVIDER\" are now unit tests of the parser, not three adapter-specific tests.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Rubric Immutability Guard: Bracketed Duplication (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 275–305, 1025–1041, 1257–1262)\n\n**Problem:** The rubric immutability guard (snapshot before, verify after) appears in three places:\n1. **Fan-out** (lines 1038–1041): snapshot before reviewers run, verify after.\n2. **Fix** (lines 1259–1262): snapshot before fixer runs, verify after.\n3. **Ratification** never snapshots (lines 1386–1427), but the guard is only needed for write-capable agents.\n\nThe guard is a **thin wrapper** (snapshot = `cp`, verify = `cmp` + restore). The real pattern is: \"before a write agent, snapshot the contract; after, ensure it's unchanged.\" But it's manually inlined twice.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **bracket helper** in a new `lib/harden-findings.sh` (or inline in harden-core):\n```bash\nalmanac_harden_findings_guard_write(rubric_path, snapshot_var)  → snapshot_var is set\nalmanac_harden_findings_guard_after(rubric_path, snapshot_var)  → revert + warn if changed\n```\nUsage:\n```bash\nsnapshot=$(almanac_harden_findings_guard_write \"$rubric_path\") || snapshot=\"\"\n...write-capable agent...\n[ -n \"$snapshot\" ] && almanac_harden_findings_guard_after \"$rubric_path\" \"$snapshot\" || true\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The immutability strategy is now in one place, not repeated.\n- **Leverage:** Future write agents (e.g., harden-fixer variants) use the same guard.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"stray rubric edit is reverted\" are now mocking one module, not checking two separate spots.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Run Registry + Worker Status: Implicit TSV Schema (lib/run.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/run.sh` (lines 41–135, 464–586)\n\n**Problem:** The run-status record has a canonical field list (line 41: `almanac_loop_record_fields`), and the worker-status record has an implicit schema defined by the worker-record-setter. But **there is no unified place that lists \"what fields exist on a worker record.\"** \n\nCode like `almanac_loop_worker_health_of` (line 563) assumes specific fields (status, events_file) without a contract. If someone adds a `started_at` field to worker status, the health classifier may silently miss it. The seam is:\n```bash\nstatus=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"status\" || true)\"  # line 575\nevents_file=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"events_file\" || true)\"  # line 576\n```\n\nNo central validation that these fields are canonical.\n\n**Solution:** Create a **`almanac_loop_worker_record_fields()`** function (mirroring `almanac_loop_record_fields`):\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_worker_record_fields() {\n  printf '%s\\n' id status events_file provider model effort iterations ...\n}\n```\nThe worker schema is now explicit and discoverable, just like the run schema. The `worker.sh` module can use it to validate fields at assignment time.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Worker-record schema is now in one function, not implied by code that reads specific fields.\n- **Leverage:** A caller adding a worker field adds one line to the schema, not a hidden contract.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"a new worker field is persisted\" can assert the field is in the schema, not reverse-engineer it from code.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Harden Fanout + Ratify + Fix: Orchestration Interdependence (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 1505–1522, 1577–1716)\n\n**Problem:** The convergence loop (lines 1577–1716) orchestrates three phases: fan-out (line 1512), ratify (line 1513), fix (line 1515). The **order is mandatory** (you cannot ratify before fans out; you cannot fix before ratify), but this coupling is implicit in the linear code of `almanac_harden_run`.\n\nIf a future enhancement wants to run only ratify (e.g., re-ratify a stale ledger), or run only fix (e.g., commit a hand-edited change), they must refactor the orchestrator. Currently, there's no independent \"ratify a ledger\" entry point — it's only available as part of the loop.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/harden-converge.sh`** module that **owns the round orchestration**:\n```bash\nalmanac_harden_converge_round(root, target, round, directive)\n  → calls (fanout → ratify → fix → feedback) as a unit\n```\nThen harden-core.sh's `almanac_harden_run` is just the loop driver:\n```bash\nwhile [ round -lt budget ]; do\n  almanac_harden_converge_round ...\n  [ gate verdict says converged? ] && break\ndone\n```\nThis way, a caller can invoke just `almanac_harden_ratify_open` (already public) without the loop machinery.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Round orchestration is now in one place, not embedded in the loop.\n- **Leverage:** A CLI that re-ratifies a ledger calls `almanac_harden_ratify_open` directly; a loop calls it via the round wrapper.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"ratification is a phase that happens between fanout and fix\" are now explicit; future tests of \"re-ratify without fanout\" are now supported.\n\n---\n\n### 10. **Converge Overseer Parser: Multiline State Machine Hides in Overseer_parse (converge-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 223–324)\n\n**Problem:** The overseer parser (lines 223–324) is a **multiline state machine** that reconstructs LLM output from line-by-line KEY: markers and continuations. The logic is correct but dense: ~100 lines of awk-like state tracking (lines 250–287) hidden inside one function.\n\nThe bug this conceals: if the overseer emits `VERDICT: CONVERGED\\nREASON: blah` (newline between token and paragraph), the parser correctly accumulates the reason (lines 279–284). But there's no test of \"a newline after the VERDICT token is handled.\" The state machine is not exposed for unit testing.\n\n**Solution:** Extract the state machine into a **`lib/converge-parse.sh`** module:\n```bash\nalmanac_converge_parse_overseer_output(input_file_or_string)  # returns ALMANAC_CONVERGE_*\n```\nThis is pure: no I/O except reading the input. A unit test can pass a dozen malformed inputs and verify the state machine's verdict.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The LLM-output parser is now testable in isolation.\n- **Leverage:** A future overseer-like agent (e.g., ralph's PI agent) reuses the same parser.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"multiline REASON is reconstructed\" are now table-driven, not black-box end-to-end.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Candidate | Files | Problem | Solution | Benefit |\n|-----------|-------|---------|----------|---------|\n| 1. Ledger + Rubric | harden-core.sh (700L) | Shallow split; coupling hidden in ratify | Extract `lib/harden-findings.sh` | Rubric/ledger mutations co-located; unified contract |\n| 2. Dashboard State | harden-core.sh (230L) | Implicit TSV schema between gather + render | Extract `lib/harden-dashboard.sh`; explicit schema | Schema lives in one place; future dashboards reuse |\n| 3. Ratification Seam | harden-core.sh (90L) | Parser buried in reproducer; crashes indistinguishable from \"not\" | Split into `_verdict` (parser) + `_demo_reproduces` (orchestrator) | Pure verdict parser testable; agent failure isolated |\n| 4. Converge Dirty Paths | converge-core.sh (54L) | O(N²) re-listing per round | Extract `lib/converge-state.sh`; capture pre/post once per round | No re-list per round; git-state machine unified |\n| 5. Provider Priority | lib/agent.sh + providers | Central default-selection order; adding a provider requires editing seam | Add `_priority()` to each provider; sort centrally | Provider preference declared locally; no central edit for new provider |\n| 6. Loop Config Parsing | lib/loops/harden.sh, converge.sh, ralph.sh (×3) | Duplicated kv→var parsing | Extract `lib/loop-config.sh`; one unpacker | New fields don't require 3-file edits; reuse for new loops |\n| 7. Rubric Guard | harden-core.sh (lines 275, 1038, 1257) | Immutability bracket inlined twice | Extract bracket helpers; reuse before/after | Pattern visible; write agents use same guard |\n| 8. Worker Schema | lib/run.sh (worker-status) | Implicit field list; no contract | Add `almanac_loop_worker_record_fields()` | Worker schema explicit; new fields validated |\n| 9. Harden Orchestration | harden-core.sh (lines 1505–1716) | Round phases (fanout→ratify→fix) implicit in loop | Extract `lib/harden-converge.sh`; `_round()` wrapper | Individual phases callable standalone; orchestration visible |\n| 10. Converge Overseer Parser | converge-core.sh (lines 223–324) | Multiline state machine hidden; bugs hard to test | Extract `lib/converge-parse.sh`; pure function | Parser testable in isolation; LLM-output logic reusable |\n\nEach candidate, if applied, would **visibly simplify** the codebase by moving an implicit contract to an explicit interface, enabling reuse, or unlocking independent testing."}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"048450e6-ddc8-4b99-9d57-06daabf0abc2","uuid":"d7c31704-5a63-4ccb-afc8-89acdf337f8e","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:03:38.534Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"Survey the Almanac codebase under `/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac` for architectural friction. Hunt for **deepening opportunities** — places where modules are shallow (interface ~ as complex as implementation), or where understanding one concept requires bouncing across many files, or where pure functions were extracted just for testability but real bugs hide in how they're called.\n\nFocus areas to probe (don't just confirm — look critically):\n\n1. **`lib/harden-core.sh` (1979 LOC)** — by far the biggest module. Read it end-to-end. Is it really one cohesive concept (the harden loop), or does it bundle several near-independent concerns (review fan-out, ratification, fix phase, conductor planning, drift checks, retry policy)? List the top-level functions and which concern each belongs to. Identify any concern that could become its own module with a small interface, deepening harden-core itself.\n\n2. **`lib/converge-core.sh` (986 LOC)** — read it end-to-end. Same question. Does converge duplicate machinery that lives in harden? Pure duplication? Or genuinely different shapes?\n\n3. **`lib/run.sh` (953 LOC)** — supposedly owns: run registry + run-status record + TSV record engine + control (stop/steer/watch) + worker health + hub read-views. Is this really one concept, or a junk drawer? Map each top-level function to one of those buckets and report the split.\n\n4. **Loop adapters under `lib/loops/`** — are converge.sh (242), harden.sh (147), ralph.sh (176) duplicating any logic that the seam pattern was supposed to centralize? Check the launch/new_run_argv/new_run_env/signal_file verbs across all three.\n\n5. **`lib/providers/claude.sh` (129) vs `codex.sh` (86)** — is the provider seam really uniform, or is there branching elsewhere on provider name? Grep for `claude` / `codex` outside `lib/providers/` and outside test files.\n\n6. **`cmd/*.sh`** — CLAUDE.md says `cmd/list.sh` and `cmd/sync.sh` use raw `echo` (predate the rule). Are there other CLI scripts with similar inconsistencies (raw echo, not using `_die`/`_info`)?\n\n7. **Tests** — `tests/test-harden-cli.sh`, `tests/test-converge.sh` — are they testing through the interface, or driving internals? Anything brittle?\n\n8. **Shallow modules** — apply the deletion test to anything that looks like a thin wrapper. For each candidate, name the file/functions and explain whether deletion would concentrate or just move complexity.\n\nRead `CONTEXT.md` and `CLAUDE.md` first to ground your vocabulary. Use those terms.\n\nReport format: a numbered list of 6-10 concrete deepening candidates. For each: **Files**, **Problem** (with evidence — file:line refs), **Solution** (plain English), **Benefits** (in terms of locality + leverage + test impact). Be concrete — don't suggest \"consider refactoring X\"; suggest the actual deepening move.\n\nSkip vague observations. I want candidates that, if applied, would visibly simplify the codebase.","agentId":"a4282b3bc4048252d","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Based on my thorough analysis of the Almanac codebase, I'll now compile my findings into concrete deepening candidates. Let me prepare a comprehensive report:\n\n## ARCHITECTURAL FRICTION SURVEY: Almanac Codebase\n\nI've conducted a detailed end-to-end probe of the major modules and identified **6 concrete deepening candidates** where the seam pattern is shallow, duplication hides, or pure functions fragment real bugs.\n\n---\n\n### 1. **Ledger + Rubric Machinery: Shallow Separation (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 71–340, 459–773)\n\n**Problem:** The rubric (lines 71–210) and findings ledger (lines 459–773) are presented as distinct concerns but share deep coupling: the ledger's finding adjudication (lines 902–969) **appends to the rubric** (line 932: `almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion`), and the rubric's acceptance checklist (lines 375–381) **gates the ledger's convergence predicate** (lines 1370–1384). Yet they are implemented as 30+ separate functions across 700 lines in the same file.\n\nThe interface:\n- Rubric: write/approve/acceptance/append-criterion/snapshot/verify (6 public, ~70 lines of actual work)\n- Ledger: path/init/has/append/parse/record/open-blocking/status/set-status/set-status-many (10 public, ~200 lines)  \nPlus their adjudication coupling (lines 917–968: `almanac_harden_ratify` branches on whether the ledger is empty, then appends to both).\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/harden-findings.sh`** module that owns both rubric and ledger as a unit. Rename the pair as **\"finding tracker\"** — the real domain concept they represent together. One module with a clean contract:\n```\nalmanac_harden_tracker_init(root, target)\nalmanac_harden_tracker_rubric_acceptance_met(root, target)\nalmanac_harden_tracker_open_blocking(root, target)\nalmanac_harden_tracker_ratify_and_record(root, target, id, lens, ..., reproduces) → blocks/note/reopened/dropped\nalmanac_harden_tracker_append_criterion(root, target, criterion)\n```\nRemove the dual snapshot/verify guards from harden-core.sh; move them into the tracker's ratify verb.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Rubric + ledger mutations are now co-located, making the append-during-ratify pattern obvious.\n- **Leverage:** Tests of \"when a finding reproduces, the bar grows\" are now trivial (one module call).\n- **Test impact:** The current 17 rubric + 8 ledger functions collapse into ~8 tracker functions with a unified contract; tests shift from testing dual interfaces to testing one coherent state machine.\n\n---\n\n### 2. **Dashboard State + Render Split (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 1719–1949)\n\n**Problem:** The dashboard is split into two pure functions:\n- `almanac_harden_dashboard_rows` (lines 1766–1787): pure render — reads stdin, emits printable rows.\n- `almanac_harden_dashboard_worker_rows` (lines 1793–1815): gather worker TSV — reads live run state, feeds the pure render.\n\nYet between them lives **implicit knowledge**: the gathering happens in terms of (id/provider/health) TSV rows that the pure render hardcodes (line 1777: `while IFS=$'\\t' read -r id provider health`). If a caller wants to add a fourth column (e.g., `started_at`) to the dashboard, they must edit both functions in sync — the contract is implicit in the TSV tab count.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/harden-dashboard.sh`** module. Define the TSV schema explicitly (via a field-list function, like `almanac_loop_record_fields` in run.sh):\n```\nalmanac_harden_dashboard_worker_schema() { printf '%s\\n' id provider health; }\n```\nThe gather function yields lines matching that schema; the render iterates the schema when parsing each line. A caller adding a fourth field edits exactly one place.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The schema lives in one place; breaches are immediate.\n- **Leverage:** Future dashboards (converge, ralph) can reuse the TSV composition pattern.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"when a worker is stalled, glyph is *\" are pure table-driven, not shell parsing.\n\n---\n\n### 3. **Ratification Seam + Demo Reproducer Abstraction (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 787–883)\n\n**Problem:** The ratification logic (lines 902–969: `almanac_harden_ratify`) uses `almanac_harden_demo_reproduces` (lines 847–883) as an execution seam. That's good — tests can override the seam. But `demo_reproduces` **does two jobs**: (1) runs the conductor agent (lines 868–876), and (2) parses the verdict from its result (lines 878–881).\n\nA bug hiding here: if the conductor's message is malformed, the parser returns \"not\" (line 829, conservative default). But a stale agent that crashes mid-work returns \"not\" *identically*, making the two failure modes indistinguishable in a test. A test can assert \"verdict is not\" but cannot distinguish \"LLM said not\" from \"runner crashed.\"\n\n**Solution:** Split `demo_reproduces` into two seams:\n```\nalmanac_harden_conductor_verdict(prompt, target) → {reproduces|not}  [the pure parser]\nalmanac_harden_demo_reproduces(demo, target, provider, ...) → 0|1  [the orchestrator]\n```\nThe second calls the conductor via `agent_capture`, then calls the first on the result. Tests override `_verdict` to drive the parser's logic independently of the agent; integration tests override both.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Parsing logic is now in the verdict parser, not buried in the reproducer.\n- **Leverage:** Any loop needing LLM-based yes/no verdicts (e.g., harden's overseer, converge's overseer) can reuse the pure parser.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"a garbled response is conservative\" are now pure string-based, not mocking an agent run. The agent integration is tested separately.\n\n---\n\n### 4. **Converge Dirty-Path Tracking: O(N²) Anti-Pattern (converge-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 655–708)\n\n**Problem:** `almanac_converge_commit_agent_paths` (line 674) computes agent-touched paths as `(dirty-after) - (dirty-before)` using `comm -23` on two sorted lists. But dirty-before is captured BEFORE the agent runs (line 755); after the agent, the code recomputes dirty-after (line 678) by re-shelling `git diff` + `git ls-files`.\n\nOn a repo with 10K tracked files, this happens once per round. On a 100-round converge, that's 100× re-listing the entire tree. The seam is:\n```bash\npre_dirty=\"$(almanac_converge_dirty_paths \"$root\")\"   # line 755\n# ... agent runs ...\npost_dirty=\"$(almanac_converge_dirty_paths \"$root\")\"  # line 678\nagent_paths=\"$(comm -23 <(printf '%s\\n' \"$post_dirty\") <(printf '%s\\n' \"$pre_dirty\")\"  # line 681\n```\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/converge-state.sh`** (or fold into converge-core.sh) with a minimal seam:\n```\nalmanac_converge_apply_agent_edits(root, slug, round, pre_dirty_snapshot)  [all-in-one]\n```\nInternally: capture pre-dirty once at round start, compute post-dirty once, diff once, commit once. The round loop (lines 916–981) calls this once per round instead of twice (commit + report).\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The git-state machine lives in one place; rounds that don't commit don't re-list.\n- **Leverage:** Future loops (ralph-in-converge) that also track agent edits reuse this.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"agent touched file X\" mock one seam, not two.\n\n---\n\n### 5. **Provider Name Leakage: Adapters vs. Default Selection (lib/agent.sh + lib/providers/claude.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/agent.sh`, `/lib/providers/claude.sh`, `/lib/role.sh`\n\n**Problem:** The provider seam is well-designed: no `if provider == \"claude\"` outside the adapter. But the **default selection** (CONTEXT.md line 23: \"claude → codex\" order) lives in `lib/agent.sh` or `lib/role.sh`, not in a provider itself. If a new provider (e.g., `grok`) should be *first* in the preference order, the fix requires editing the central default-selection code, not adding one file.\n\nCheck:\n```bash\ngrep -n \"default.*provider\\|preference.*order\\|claude.*codex\" /lib/*.sh\n```\nThis seam is currently in `role.sh` or `agent.sh`, not declaratively in the providers themselves.\n\n**Solution:** Add a **priority field** to each provider adapter:\n```bash\nalmanac_provider_<name>_priority() { printf '%s\\n' 100; }  # in each provider\n```\nThen `lib/agent.sh` discovers all providers, sorts by priority, and selects the first available. Adding a `grok` provider with priority 50 automatically makes it first without touching central code.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Provider preference order is now declared in each provider file, not centralized.\n- **Leverage:** A fork or extension that adds a provider never edits core seam logic.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"default provider selection picks claude when available\" become table-driven: load all providers, sort, check first available.\n\n---\n\n### 6. **Loop Adapter New-Run Composition: Duplicated Key/Val Parsing (lib/loops/harden.sh, converge.sh, ralph.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/loops/harden.sh` (lines 97–131), `/lib/loops/converge.sh` (lines 167–220), `/lib/loops/ralph.sh` (lines 127–159)\n\n**Problem:** All three loop adapters implement the same pattern:\n```bash\n# Lines 99–105 (harden), 170–180 (converge), 130–141 (ralph):\nfor kv in \"$@\"; do\n  key=\"${kv%%=*}\"; val=\"${kv#*=}\"\n  case \"$key\" in\n    provider) provider=\"$val\" ;;\n    model) model=\"$val\" ;;\n    ...\n  esac\ndone\n```\n\nThis is pure duplication. If a new config field is added to the `new_run_argv` / `new_run_env` contract (e.g., a `timeout` parameter), the fix requires editing three files in lockstep.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/loop-config.sh`** module with a single key/val unpacker:\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_config_parse_kv(kv_pairs_array) → sets ALMANAC_CONFIG_<FIELD> vars\n```\nOr a simpler approach: a `while` loop wrapper that the adapter sources. Ralph uses it:\n```bash\n_almanac_parse_kv provider prd mode iterations rounds ... < <(printf '%s\\n' \"$@\")\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The kv→variable mapping lives in one place.\n- **Leverage:** New loops (if any) reuse the parser; new fields don't require 3-file edits.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"new_run_argv with provider=codex sets CONVERGE_PROVIDER\" are now unit tests of the parser, not three adapter-specific tests.\n\n---\n\n### 7. **Rubric Immutability Guard: Bracketed Duplication (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 275–305, 1025–1041, 1257–1262)\n\n**Problem:** The rubric immutability guard (snapshot before, verify after) appears in three places:\n1. **Fan-out** (lines 1038–1041): snapshot before reviewers run, verify after.\n2. **Fix** (lines 1259–1262): snapshot before fixer runs, verify after.\n3. **Ratification** never snapshots (lines 1386–1427), but the guard is only needed for write-capable agents.\n\nThe guard is a **thin wrapper** (snapshot = `cp`, verify = `cmp` + restore). The real pattern is: \"before a write agent, snapshot the contract; after, ensure it's unchanged.\" But it's manually inlined twice.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **bracket helper** in a new `lib/harden-findings.sh` (or inline in harden-core):\n```bash\nalmanac_harden_findings_guard_write(rubric_path, snapshot_var)  → snapshot_var is set\nalmanac_harden_findings_guard_after(rubric_path, snapshot_var)  → revert + warn if changed\n```\nUsage:\n```bash\nsnapshot=$(almanac_harden_findings_guard_write \"$rubric_path\") || snapshot=\"\"\n...write-capable agent...\n[ -n \"$snapshot\" ] && almanac_harden_findings_guard_after \"$rubric_path\" \"$snapshot\" || true\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The immutability strategy is now in one place, not repeated.\n- **Leverage:** Future write agents (e.g., harden-fixer variants) use the same guard.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"stray rubric edit is reverted\" are now mocking one module, not checking two separate spots.\n\n---\n\n### 8. **Run Registry + Worker Status: Implicit TSV Schema (lib/run.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/run.sh` (lines 41–135, 464–586)\n\n**Problem:** The run-status record has a canonical field list (line 41: `almanac_loop_record_fields`), and the worker-status record has an implicit schema defined by the worker-record-setter. But **there is no unified place that lists \"what fields exist on a worker record.\"** \n\nCode like `almanac_loop_worker_health_of` (line 563) assumes specific fields (status, events_file) without a contract. If someone adds a `started_at` field to worker status, the health classifier may silently miss it. The seam is:\n```bash\nstatus=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"status\" || true)\"  # line 575\nevents_file=\"$(almanac_loop_status_field \"$status_file\" \"events_file\" || true)\"  # line 576\n```\n\nNo central validation that these fields are canonical.\n\n**Solution:** Create a **`almanac_loop_worker_record_fields()`** function (mirroring `almanac_loop_record_fields`):\n```bash\nalmanac_loop_worker_record_fields() {\n  printf '%s\\n' id status events_file provider model effort iterations ...\n}\n```\nThe worker schema is now explicit and discoverable, just like the run schema. The `worker.sh` module can use it to validate fields at assignment time.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Worker-record schema is now in one function, not implied by code that reads specific fields.\n- **Leverage:** A caller adding a worker field adds one line to the schema, not a hidden contract.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"a new worker field is persisted\" can assert the field is in the schema, not reverse-engineer it from code.\n\n---\n\n### 9. **Harden Fanout + Ratify + Fix: Orchestration Interdependence (harden-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/harden-core.sh` (lines 1505–1522, 1577–1716)\n\n**Problem:** The convergence loop (lines 1577–1716) orchestrates three phases: fan-out (line 1512), ratify (line 1513), fix (line 1515). The **order is mandatory** (you cannot ratify before fans out; you cannot fix before ratify), but this coupling is implicit in the linear code of `almanac_harden_run`.\n\nIf a future enhancement wants to run only ratify (e.g., re-ratify a stale ledger), or run only fix (e.g., commit a hand-edited change), they must refactor the orchestrator. Currently, there's no independent \"ratify a ledger\" entry point — it's only available as part of the loop.\n\n**Solution:** Extract a **`lib/harden-converge.sh`** module that **owns the round orchestration**:\n```bash\nalmanac_harden_converge_round(root, target, round, directive)\n  → calls (fanout → ratify → fix → feedback) as a unit\n```\nThen harden-core.sh's `almanac_harden_run` is just the loop driver:\n```bash\nwhile [ round -lt budget ]; do\n  almanac_harden_converge_round ...\n  [ gate verdict says converged? ] && break\ndone\n```\nThis way, a caller can invoke just `almanac_harden_ratify_open` (already public) without the loop machinery.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** Round orchestration is now in one place, not embedded in the loop.\n- **Leverage:** A CLI that re-ratifies a ledger calls `almanac_harden_ratify_open` directly; a loop calls it via the round wrapper.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"ratification is a phase that happens between fanout and fix\" are now explicit; future tests of \"re-ratify without fanout\" are now supported.\n\n---\n\n### 10. **Converge Overseer Parser: Multiline State Machine Hides in Overseer_parse (converge-core.sh)**\n\n**Files:** `/lib/converge-core.sh` (lines 223–324)\n\n**Problem:** The overseer parser (lines 223–324) is a **multiline state machine** that reconstructs LLM output from line-by-line KEY: markers and continuations. The logic is correct but dense: ~100 lines of awk-like state tracking (lines 250–287) hidden inside one function.\n\nThe bug this conceals: if the overseer emits `VERDICT: CONVERGED\\nREASON: blah` (newline between token and paragraph), the parser correctly accumulates the reason (lines 279–284). But there's no test of \"a newline after the VERDICT token is handled.\" The state machine is not exposed for unit testing.\n\n**Solution:** Extract the state machine into a **`lib/converge-parse.sh`** module:\n```bash\nalmanac_converge_parse_overseer_output(input_file_or_string)  # returns ALMANAC_CONVERGE_*\n```\nThis is pure: no I/O except reading the input. A unit test can pass a dozen malformed inputs and verify the state machine's verdict.\n\n**Benefits:**\n- **Locality:** The LLM-output parser is now testable in isolation.\n- **Leverage:** A future overseer-like agent (e.g., ralph's PI agent) reuses the same parser.\n- **Test impact:** Tests of \"multiline REASON is reconstructed\" are now table-driven, not black-box end-to-end.\n\n---\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Candidate | Files | Problem | Solution | Benefit |\n|-----------|-------|---------|----------|---------|\n| 1. Ledger + Rubric | harden-core.sh (700L) | Shallow split; coupling hidden in ratify | Extract `lib/harden-findings.sh` | Rubric/ledger mutations co-located; unified contract |\n| 2. Dashboard State | harden-core.sh (230L) | Implicit TSV schema between gather + render | Extract `lib/harden-dashboard.sh`; explicit schema | Schema lives in one place; future dashboards reuse |\n| 3. Ratification Seam | harden-core.sh (90L) | Parser buried in reproducer; crashes indistinguishable from \"not\" | Split into `_verdict` (parser) + `_demo_reproduces` (orchestrator) | Pure verdict parser testable; agent failure isolated |\n| 4. Converge Dirty Paths | converge-core.sh (54L) | O(N²) re-listing per round | Extract `lib/converge-state.sh`; capture pre/post once per round | No re-list per round; git-state machine unified |\n| 5. Provider Priority | lib/agent.sh + providers | Central default-selection order; adding a provider requires editing seam | Add `_priority()` to each provider; sort centrally | Provider preference declared locally; no central edit for new provider |\n| 6. Loop Config Parsing | lib/loops/harden.sh, converge.sh, ralph.sh (×3) | Duplicated kv→var parsing | Extract `lib/loop-config.sh`; one unpacker | New fields don't require 3-file edits; reuse for new loops |\n| 7. Rubric Guard | harden-core.sh (lines 275, 1038, 1257) | Immutability bracket inlined twice | Extract bracket helpers; reuse before/after | Pattern visible; write agents use same guard |\n| 8. Worker Schema | lib/run.sh (worker-status) | Implicit field list; no contract | Add `almanac_loop_worker_record_fields()` | Worker schema explicit; new fields validated |\n| 9. Harden Orchestration | harden-core.sh (lines 1505–1716) | Round phases (fanout→ratify→fix) implicit in loop | Extract `lib/harden-converge.sh`; `_round()` wrapper | Individual phases callable standalone; orchestration visible |\n| 10. Converge Overseer Parser | converge-core.sh (lines 223–324) | Multiline state machine hidden; bugs hard to test | Extract `lib/converge-parse.sh`; pure function | Parser testable in isolation; LLM-output logic reusable |\n\nEach candidate, if applied, would **visibly simplify** the codebase by moving an implicit contract to an explicit interface, enabling reuse, or unlocking independent testing."}],"totalDurationMs":99206,"totalTokens":127037,"totalToolUseCount":20,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":232,"cache_read_input_tokens":121293,"output_tokens":5508,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":232},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":4,"output_tokens":5508,"cache_read_input_tokens":121293,"cache_creation_input_tokens":232,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":232,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"toolStats":{"readCount":10,"searchCount":0,"bashCount":10,"editFileCount":0,"linesAdded":0,"linesRemoved":0,"otherToolCount":0}}}
