{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Gqg5ommtKPAyz6v1vedFs7","type":"tool_result","content":"260\t# --- Rubric immutability guard -------------------------------------------------\n261\t#\n262\t# The rubric is the contract, and during a run it is immutable to agents: the\n263\t# goalposts move only by a deliberate human act (the developer editing the file\n264\t# between runs / approving it) or the conductor's controlled append at\n265\t# ratification (almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion — a visible, monotonic\n266\t# diff that runs OUTSIDE the reviewer/fixer phase). Reviewers run read-only and\n267\t# cannot write the rubric; the fixer (later slice) has write access and could.\n268\t# These two helpers bracket an agent phase — snapshot before, verify after — and\n269\t# revert any agent edit, so a stray agent write during a run cannot lower the bar.\n270\t\n271\t# Snapshot the rubric to a protected temp copy and echo the snapshot path so the\n272\t# caller can pass it to almanac_harden_rubric_verify. Returns 2 (echoes nothing)\n273\t# when no rubric exists — an ad-hoc bare run with no drafted contract has nothing\n274\t# to guard.\n275\talmanac_harden_rubric_snapshot() {\n276\t  local rubric_path=\"$1\"\n277\t  local snap\n278\t\n279\t  [ -f \"$rubric_path\" ] || return 2\n280\t\n281\t  snap=\"$(mktemp \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/almanac-rubric-lock.XXXXXX\")\"\n282\t  cp \"$rubric_path\" \"$snap\"\n283\t  printf '%s\\n' \"$snap\"\n284\t}\n285\t\n286\t# Verify the rubric is byte-identical to its snapshot; if an agent modified it\n287\t# during the guarded phase, revert it and warn (the contract is immutable to\n288\t# agents). Discards the snapshot either way. Returns 1 when a modification was\n289\t# reverted, 0 when unchanged, 2 when the snapshot is missing.\n290\talmanac_harden_rubric_verify() {\n291\t  local rubric_path=\"$1\"\n292\t  local snap=\"$2\"\n293\t\n294\t  [ -n \"$snap\" ] && [ -f \"$snap\" ] || return 2\n295\t\n296\t  if ! cmp -s \"$rubric_path\" \"$snap\"; then\n297\t    cp \"$snap\" \"$rubric_path\"\n298\t    rm -f \"$snap\"\n299\t    _warn \"Rubric was modified during the run; reverted (contract is immutable to agents — amend it directly between runs).\"\n300\t    return 1\n301\t  fi\n302\t\n303\t  rm -f \"$snap\"\n304\t  return 0\n305\t}\n306\t\n307\t# Emit the configured reviewer lens set, one lens per line. Defaults to the five\n308\t# PRD lenses; override at runtime via HARDEN_LENSES (comma- or whitespace-\n309\t# separated).\n310\talmanac_harden_lenses() {\n311\t  local raw=\"${HARDEN_LENSES:-correctness security perf edge-cases contracts}\"\n312\t\n313\t  # Trailing newline matters: a `read` loop consumer drops a final line that\n314\t  # lacks one, which would silently swallow the last lens.\n315\t  printf '%s\\n' \"$raw\" | tr -s ', \\t' '\\n' | sed '/^$/d'\n316\t}\n317\t\n318\talmanac_harden_max_reviewers() {\n319\t  printf '%s\\n' \"${HARDEN_MAX_REVIEWERS:-16}\"\n320\t}\n321\t\n322\t# Build the fixed prompt for one read-only reviewer over a target, including the\n323\t# JSON-Lines findings schema. When a rubric path is given and exists, the rubric's\n324\t# acceptance criteria are embedded as the bar the reviewer judges against, so\n325\t# reviewers consume the contract rather than an implicit standard. An optional\n326\t# steering directive (4th arg) is embedded too, so an operator who redirects the\n327\t# run at the HITL checkpoint (e.g. \"focus on the auth module\") reaches the\n328\t# reviewers on the next round. Kept separate so the schema can grow without\n329\t# touching orchestration. (Exact prompt wording is not asserted in tests by\n330\t# design; that the rubric bar and the steer directive are consumed is.)\n331\talmanac_harden_reviewer_prompt() {\n332\t  local target=\"$1\"\n333\t  local lens=\"${2:-correctness}\"\n334\t  local rubric_path=\"${3:-}\"\n335\t  local directive=\"${4:-}\"\n336\t  local rubric_bar=\"\"\n337\t\n338\t  if [ -n \"$rubric_path\" ] && [ -f \"$rubric_path\" ]; then\n339\t    rubric_bar=\"$(almanac_harden_rubric_acceptance \"$rubric_path\")\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"048450e6-ddc8-4b99-9d57-06daabf0abc2","uuid":"f10b6fb9-15e5-4cda-9e4d-3524d83b765e","timestamp":"2026-05-27T06:04:53.005Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/jakubneumann/Documents/code/neumie/almanac/lib/harden-core.sh","content":"# --- Rubric immutability guard -------------------------------------------------\n#\n# The rubric is the contract, and during a run it is immutable to agents: the\n# goalposts move only by a deliberate human act (the developer editing the file\n# between runs / approving it) or the conductor's controlled append at\n# ratification (almanac_harden_rubric_append_criterion — a visible, monotonic\n# diff that runs OUTSIDE the reviewer/fixer phase). Reviewers run read-only and\n# cannot write the rubric; the fixer (later slice) has write access and could.\n# These two helpers bracket an agent phase — snapshot before, verify after — and\n# revert any agent edit, so a stray agent write during a run cannot lower the bar.\n\n# Snapshot the rubric to a protected temp copy and echo the snapshot path so the\n# caller can pass it to almanac_harden_rubric_verify. Returns 2 (echoes nothing)\n# when no rubric exists — an ad-hoc bare run with no drafted contract has nothing\n# to guard.\nalmanac_harden_rubric_snapshot() {\n  local rubric_path=\"$1\"\n  local snap\n\n  [ -f \"$rubric_path\" ] || return 2\n\n  snap=\"$(mktemp \"${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/almanac-rubric-lock.XXXXXX\")\"\n  cp \"$rubric_path\" \"$snap\"\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$snap\"\n}\n\n# Verify the rubric is byte-identical to its snapshot; if an agent modified it\n# during the guarded phase, revert it and warn (the contract is immutable to\n# agents). Discards the snapshot either way. Returns 1 when a modification was\n# reverted, 0 when unchanged, 2 when the snapshot is missing.\nalmanac_harden_rubric_verify() {\n  local rubric_path=\"$1\"\n  local snap=\"$2\"\n\n  [ -n \"$snap\" ] && [ -f \"$snap\" ] || return 2\n\n  if ! cmp -s \"$rubric_path\" \"$snap\"; then\n    cp \"$snap\" \"$rubric_path\"\n    rm -f \"$snap\"\n    _warn \"Rubric was modified during the run; reverted (contract is immutable to agents — amend it directly between runs).\"\n    return 1\n  fi\n\n  rm -f \"$snap\"\n  return 0\n}\n\n# Emit the configured reviewer lens set, one lens per line. Defaults to the five\n# PRD lenses; override at runtime via HARDEN_LENSES (comma- or whitespace-\n# separated).\nalmanac_harden_lenses() {\n  local raw=\"${HARDEN_LENSES:-correctness security perf edge-cases contracts}\"\n\n  # Trailing newline matters: a `read` loop consumer drops a final line that\n  # lacks one, which would silently swallow the last lens.\n  printf '%s\\n' \"$raw\" | tr -s ', \\t' '\\n' | sed '/^$/d'\n}\n\nalmanac_harden_max_reviewers() {\n  printf '%s\\n' \"${HARDEN_MAX_REVIEWERS:-16}\"\n}\n\n# Build the fixed prompt for one read-only reviewer over a target, including the\n# JSON-Lines findings schema. When a rubric path is given and exists, the rubric's\n# acceptance criteria are embedded as the bar the reviewer judges against, so\n# reviewers consume the contract rather than an implicit standard. An optional\n# steering directive (4th arg) is embedded too, so an operator who redirects the\n# run at the HITL checkpoint (e.g. \"focus on the auth module\") reaches the\n# reviewers on the next round. Kept separate so the schema can grow without\n# touching orchestration. (Exact prompt wording is not asserted in tests by\n# design; that the rubric bar and the steer directive are consumed is.)\nalmanac_harden_reviewer_prompt() {\n  local target=\"$1\"\n  local lens=\"${2:-correctness}\"\n  local rubric_path=\"${3:-}\"\n  local directive=\"${4:-}\"\n  local rubric_bar=\"\"\n\n  if [ -n \"$rubric_path\" ] && [ -f \"$rubric_path\" ]; then\n    rubric_bar=\"$(almanac_harden_rubric_acceptance \"$rubric_path\")\"","numLines":80,"startLine":260,"totalLines":1980}}}
